drm/mediatek: support the MT6592 display pipe

MT6592's DDP is the MT2701/MT8173 generation, and almost all of what this
board needs is already here.  Verified against the MVII bootloader that
lights this panel today, register by register: MMSYS at 0x14000000, OVL0
at 0x14007000, RDMA0 at 0x14008000, COLOR0 at 0x1400b000, DSI0 at
0x1400c000, MUTEX at 0x1400e000, and every offset inside those blocks
matches the mt2701 tables in this driver.  So MMSYS, MUTEX, RDMA, COLOR,
DSI and the MIPI-TX PHY need no code at all -- the device tree gives each
node "mediatek,mt6592-<block>" followed by "mediatek,mt2701-<block>",
__of_match_node() scores the first compatible higher where a mt6592 entry
exists and falls through to the second where none does, and those five
drivers bind unchanged on driver data that is already exact:

  - mt2701_mutex_mod[] gives OVL0 = 3, COLOR0 = 7, RDMA0 = 10, so the
    mutex this driver programs for the path below is MOD = 0x488 and
    SOF = MUTEX_SOF_DSI0 = 1.  The bootloader's own MUTEX0_MOD is 0x488
    and its MUTEX0_SOF is 1.
  - mt2701_rdma_driver_data.fifo_size is SZ_4K, and SZ_4K is what the
    stock 3.4 kernel measures: its RDMA config writes 0x81000010 to
    RDMA_FIFO_CON (0x14008040), whose pseudo-size field is
    (4096 / 16) << 16.
  - mt2701_dsi_driver_data's reg_cmdq_off (0x180) and reg_vm_cmd_off
    (0x130) are the bootloader's, and reg_shadow_dbg_off is never read
    because has_shadow_ctl is false.
  - mt2701_color_driver_data.color_offset is DISP_COLOR_START_MT2701,
    which is the bootloader's COLOR start offset.

Three things are genuinely this board's own, and they are the whole patch.

1. The path.

   mt2701_mtk_ddp_main[] is OVL0, RDMA0, COLOR0, BLS, DSI0.  This board's
   is OVL0, RDMA0, COLOR0, DSI0, with the BLS used only as the backlight
   PWM and deliberately outside the mutex -- see the comment on
   mt6592_mtk_ddp_main[] for why that also means no mt6592 row is needed
   in mmsys_default_routing_table[], and why the one mux write this path
   performs is byte for byte the one the bootloader already made.

2. The OVL colour format enumeration.

   MT6592 numbers OVL_CON's input formats RGB565 = 0, RGB888 = 1,
   ARGB8888 = 2, which is what fmt_rgb565_is_0 selects.  MT2701 numbers
   the first two the other way round, so mt2701_ovl_driver_data cannot be
   reused as is even though every other field of it is right.

3. Not tearing the pipe down, which is preserve_lk_state.

   The bootloader lights this panel and hands it over running, and there
   is no cold start anywhere in this kernel to put it back: the panel
   driver adopts a live panel and all four of its power callbacks are
   empty by design.  So mtk_crtc_atomic_disable() is a one-way door here.
   It is reached whenever the framebuffer a CRTC is scanning out goes
   away -- drm_fb_release() removes a client's framebuffers when it
   closes the device, and removing one from under an active CRTC disables
   the CRTC -- and with CONFIG_DRM_FBDEV_EMULATION=n there is no
   in-kernel client left for drm_client_dev_restore() to hand the pipe
   back to.  One GL client that ran once cost the display until reboot.

   The drm_WARN "vblank wait timed out on crtc 0" that this board printed
   on every such exit is the same event seen from the other side.  That
   wait exists to let the just-disabled planes reach the hardware at the
   next SOF; a pipe being taken down underneath it does not produce one
   inside the 100 ms drm_crtc_wait_one_vblank() allows.

   With preserve_lk_state the CRTC disable does neither: no plane
   teardown, no vblank wait, no mtk_crtc_ddp_hw_fini().  The DDP, the DSI
   and the panel stay as the bootloader left them, and a later client
   gets mtk_crtc_ddp_hw_init() over a live pipe -- which is exactly what
   the first modeset after boot already does.

   Leaving the pipe up is only half of it, because the commit that
   disables the CRTC also turns every plane off, and an overlay with no
   layers scans out an opaque background that nothing can paint over
   again.  So the overlay keeps a copy of the programming it found at
   probe -- taken there because the first modeset's mtk_ovl_config()
   pulses OVL_RST and wipes it -- and mtk_crtc_atomic_flush() puts that
   copy back once the CRTC has gone inactive.  What returns is the
   bootloader's own framebuffer, which is the buffer simple-framebuffer
   and everything writing through /dev/fb0 have been drawing into all
   along.  A CRTC that has been used and released costs the display
   nothing.

   The snapshot is marked valid only where the block really was running
   at probe, so a board that boots its own display saves nothing and
   restores nothing.  The flag is on mt6592_mmsys_driver_data only, and
   every other SoC keeps the existing path byte for byte.

One measured difference is recorded here rather than changed.  The stock
kernel writes 0x20402040 to DISP_RDMA_MEM_GMC_SETTING_0 (0x14008030);
this driver writes the RDMA_MEM_GMC constant 0x40402020 there on every
SoC, MT2701 and MT8173 included.  The two differ in the ultra/pre-ultra
threshold bytes.  Nothing suggests the mainline value is wrong for this
part -- it drives the same block on the same generation -- so it is left
alone, but it is the first register to look at if the panel shows RDMA
underflow lines under load.

The interrupt numbers the new device tree nodes carry were taken from the
stock kernel too, not derived from mainline's mt6592.dtsi: its "mtk_disp"
platform_driver at 0xc0b4fc70 gives a probe at 0xc035bd24 that calls
request_threaded_irq() eight times, and the shared handler's jump table
identifies each one by the register base its branch reads.  RDMA0 = 216,
OVL0 = 217, RDMA1 = 218, BLS = 219, COLOR0 = 220, DSI0 = 221, DPI0 = 222,
MUTEX = 225.  Those are GIC INTIDs, so the device tree's GIC_SPI cell is
each one less 32.

Applies to linux-6.12.y.
---
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/mediatek/mtk_crtc.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/mediatek/mtk_crtc.c
index aa8e6f6dd..a4761d2bc 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/mediatek/mtk_crtc.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/mediatek/mtk_crtc.c
@@ -802,12 +802,52 @@ static void mtk_crtc_atomic_disable(struct drm_crtc *crtc,
 {
 	struct mtk_crtc *mtk_crtc = to_mtk_crtc(crtc);
 	struct mtk_ddp_comp *comp = mtk_crtc->ddp_comp[0];
+	struct mtk_drm_private *priv = crtc->dev->dev_private;
 	int i;
 
 	DRM_DEBUG_DRIVER("%s %d\n", __func__, crtc->base.id);
 	if (!mtk_crtc->enabled)
 		return;
 
+	/*
+	 * preserve_lk_state: leave the pipe running and hand nothing back.
+	 *
+	 * This function is reached whenever the framebuffer a CRTC is scanning
+	 * out goes away -- drm_fb_release() removes a client's framebuffers when
+	 * it closes the device, and removing one from under an active CRTC
+	 * disables the CRTC.  Where CONFIG_DRM_FBDEV_EMULATION=n there is no
+	 * in-kernel client left for drm_client_dev_restore() to hand the pipe to
+	 * afterwards, so on a board whose panel was brought up by the bootloader
+	 * and has no cold-start path here, everything below is a one-way door:
+	 * mtk_crtc_ddp_hw_fini() stops OVL, RDMA, COLOR and DSI, disconnects the
+	 * mmsys routing and disables the mutex, and nothing puts the panel back
+	 * until the next boot.  One client that ran once costs the display.
+	 *
+	 * The vblank wait further down is the other half of the same problem.
+	 * It is there to let the disabled planes reach the hardware at the next
+	 * SOF, and drm_crtc_wait_one_vblank() drm_WARNs "vblank wait timed out
+	 * on crtc 0" when that SOF does not arrive inside 100 ms -- which is
+	 * exactly what a pipe being taken down underneath it looks like.
+	 *
+	 * So do neither.  The planes are still disabled immediately afterwards
+	 * by mtk_crtc_atomic_flush(), which leaves the OVL showing its opaque
+	 * black background and not a freed buffer; the DDP, the DSI and the
+	 * panel stay as the bootloader left them; and the next client to modeset
+	 * gets mtk_crtc_ddp_hw_init() over a live pipe, which is precisely what
+	 * the FIRST modeset after boot already does and is known to work.
+	 *
+	 * drm_crtc_vblank_off() and mtk_ddp_comp_power_off() are kept because
+	 * they are bookkeeping rather than teardown: the first stops DRM
+	 * accounting vblanks for a CRTC it now believes is off, and the second
+	 * balances the reference mtk_crtc_atomic_enable() took.
+	 */
+	if (priv->data->preserve_lk_state) {
+		drm_crtc_vblank_off(crtc);
+		mtk_ddp_comp_power_off(comp);
+		mtk_crtc->enabled = false;
+		return;
+	}
+
 	/* Set all pending plane state to disabled */
 	for (i = 0; i < mtk_crtc->layer_nr; i++) {
 		struct drm_plane *plane = &mtk_crtc->planes[i];
@@ -865,6 +905,8 @@ static void mtk_crtc_atomic_flush(struct drm_crtc *crtc,
 				  struct drm_atomic_state *state)
 {
 	struct mtk_crtc *mtk_crtc = to_mtk_crtc(crtc);
+	struct mtk_drm_private *priv = crtc->dev->dev_private;
+	struct drm_crtc_state *new_state;
 	int i;
 
 	if (crtc->state->color_mgmt_changed)
@@ -873,6 +915,36 @@ static void mtk_crtc_atomic_flush(struct drm_crtc *crtc,
 			mtk_ddp_ctm_set(mtk_crtc->ddp_comp[i], crtc->state);
 		}
 	mtk_crtc_update_config(mtk_crtc, !!mtk_crtc->event);
+
+	/*
+	 * The commit above has just turned every plane off, because this is the
+	 * flush of the same commit whose mtk_crtc_atomic_disable() declined to
+	 * tear the pipe down -- see the comment there.  On a preserve_lk_state
+	 * SoC the pipe is still running, so what it shows now matters: the
+	 * client's framebuffer is about to be freed and reused, and an overlay
+	 * with no layers shows an opaque black screen that nothing can paint
+	 * over again.
+	 *
+	 * So give the panel back to whoever had it before DRM did.  The overlay
+	 * keeps a copy of the programming it found at probe, and putting that
+	 * back restores the bootloader's framebuffer -- which is the buffer the
+	 * simple-framebuffer fbdev and everything drawing through /dev/fb0 have
+	 * been writing into all along.  A CRTC that has been used and released
+	 * therefore costs the display nothing.
+	 *
+	 * new_state->active rather than mtk_crtc->enabled, because this flush
+	 * also runs BEFORE mtk_crtc_atomic_enable() on the commit that turns the
+	 * CRTC on, and restoring there would fight that client's first frame.
+	 */
+	if (!priv->data->preserve_lk_state)
+		return;
+
+	new_state = drm_atomic_get_new_crtc_state(state, crtc);
+	if (new_state && !new_state->active) {
+		mtk_mutex_acquire(mtk_crtc->mutex);
+		mtk_ddp_comp_restore_boot_layer(mtk_crtc->ddp_comp[0]);
+		mtk_mutex_release(mtk_crtc->mutex);
+	}
 }
 
 static const struct drm_crtc_funcs mtk_crtc_funcs = {
